        <div id="what-loads-the-elbow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Loads the Elbow
          </h2>

          <p>
            Several things add up to the load on a sore elbow, and the paddle affects only some of them. The first is shock and vibration at contact. Every time the ball hits the face, a jolt travels down the handle into your hand and forearm. Stiffer, thinner paddles pass more of that along, and off-center hits, especially near the edge, make it worse because the paddle twists in your hand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is grip pressure. Squeezing the handle hard keeps the forearm muscles contracted and stiffens the path from the paddle to the tendon, so more vibration reaches it. Players squeeze for three main reasons: the grip is too small, the paddle is too heavy or head-heavy to control, or they are tense. Fix the first two with equipment and the third with technique.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is weight and swing weight. A very heavy or head-heavy paddle makes the wrist extensors work harder to decelerate it after every shot. A very light paddle can push you to swing harder and tense up to generate pace. Most guidance from paddle retailers and coaches points to the light-to-medium middle ground, roughly 7.5 to 8.2 ounces, with a moderate swing weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fourth is technique: wristy flicks, late contact, hitting with an arm instead of the body, and backhands led by the elbow all increase the strain on the tendon. Most sports medicine guidance treats tennis elbow as a load problem, so the goal is to reduce the total load while the tendon recovers, and equipment is only one lever. The technique section below covers the others.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="core" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Core Thickness and Softness
          </h2>

          <p>
            The core is the biggest equipment factor in how a paddle feels on the arm. Most paddles use a polymer or polypropylene honeycomb core between 13 and 16mm thick, and a few go thicker. Thicker cores are generally described as softer and more cushioned, and they hold the ball a fraction longer, which spreads out the impact. Thinner cores are livelier and stiffer, giving more pop and more shock.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a sore elbow, 16mm is the sensible floor and thicker is often better. That is why our top pick uses a 20mm core and three others use about 16mm. The exception is the Paddletek Tempest Wave Pro-C at 14.3mm, which earns its place because of a famously soft feel that reviewers have described for years. Core thickness is a strong guide, but the material and construction matter too, as our comparison of <a href="/14mm-vs-16mm-pickleball-paddle/">14mm vs 16mm pickleball paddles</a> explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Construction affects shock as well. Several arm-health guides note that some modern foam-core and thermoformed paddles can feel firmer and transmit more shock than traditional honeycomb designs, even when they are sold on power. If you are considering a thermoformed paddle, read our explainer on <a href="/thermoformed-pickleball-paddle/">thermoformed pickleball paddles</a> and look for owner reports from players with arm issues before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Face material plays a smaller role. Fiberglass and composite faces are often described as livelier, while carbon faces are often described as more controlled and muted. Our guide to <a href="/carbon-fiber-vs-fiberglass-pickleball-paddle/">carbon fiber vs fiberglass pickleball paddles</a> covers the differences, but for elbow comfort the core matters more than the skin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Weight and Swing Weight
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paddle weight is a balancing act for a sore elbow. Heavier paddles are more stable and absorb more of the ball's energy, so less of the impact passes into the arm, but they take more effort to swing and slow down. Lighter paddles are easier to move, but can tempt you to swing harder and grip tighter for power. That is why most guidance for arm comfort settles on the light-to-medium range.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough guide, many players with elbow pain do well between about 7.5 and 8.2 ounces. Within that range, pick the heavier end if you mostly suffer on hard volleys and blocks, where stability matters, and the lighter end if you tire over long sessions. Our guide to <a href="/pickleball-paddle-weight/">pickleball paddle weight</a> covers how to choose and how lead tape changes balance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Swing weight, how heavy the paddle feels in motion, matters as much as the number on the scale. Elongated paddles and paddles with weight near the tip have higher swing weight, which makes the forearm work harder to accelerate and stop them. If your elbow hurts, a standard or hybrid shape with a moderate swing weight is often kinder than an elongated paddle at the same static weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid adding lots of lead tape to the head of a paddle to make it more stable. A little at the sides can widen the sweet spot, but tape at the tip raises swing weight quickly. If you want more stability, choose a paddle that is built heavier in the handle and body rather than making the head heavier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Grip Size and Overgrips
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grip size is the cheapest and most overlooked fix for elbow pain. A grip that is too small forces you to squeeze to stop the paddle twisting in your hand, and that squeeze is a direct load on the tendon. Many arm-health guides suggest grips of 4.125 inches or larger for players with elbow issues, and our picks sit between about 4.125 and 4.25 inches.
          </p>

          <p>
            To check fit, hold the paddle in a continental grip and see whether you can fit the index finger of your other hand in the gap between your fingertips and the base of your palm. If there is no gap, the grip is probably too small. If the gap is much wider than a finger, it may be too large. Our guide to <a href="/pickleball-paddle-grip-size/">pickleball paddle grip size</a> walks through measuring your hand properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            An overgrip adds roughly a sixteenth of an inch of circumference and a softer, tackier surface, which helps you hold the paddle with less pressure. A cushioned replacement grip under an overgrip adds more. This is often the single best change for a paddle you already own, and it costs a few dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            When in doubt, go slightly larger. A grip that is a little big is easier to live with than one that is too small, and you can always build up a small grip with an overgrip, but you cannot easily slim down one that is too large.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Technique Changes That Help
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grip pressure comes first. Hold the paddle loosely, around three or four on a ten-point scale, and only firm up slightly at contact. A relaxed hand lets the paddle absorb more of the impact and stops the forearm muscles staying tense all game. Many players with elbow pain find this change alone makes a difference within a few sessions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hit with the body, not the arm. Turn your shoulders and use your legs on drives and serves rather than whipping the wrist. On the backhand, a two-handed stroke or a shoulder-led one-handed stroke spreads the load away from the elbow. Wristy flicks and late, arm-only swings are common culprits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make contact in front. Hitting the ball late, beside or behind your body, forces the wrist and forearm to compensate. Meeting it slightly in front lets the paddle face do the work and keeps the wrist in a stronger position. Center contact matters too, because off-center hits twist the paddle and jolt the arm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manage the load. Play shorter sessions, warm up the forearm before you play, and avoid long drilling sessions that hammer the same stroke. A coach can spot the habits that load the elbow far faster than you can find them on your own, and a physical therapist can give you strengthening exercises for the forearm, which is where much of the long-term improvement comes from.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-paddle-wont-fix" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When a Paddle Won't Fix It
          </h2>

          <p>
            A softer paddle reduces the load on a sore tendon, but it does not repair it. If you keep playing through pain with the same technique and the same volume, a new paddle may only buy a little time. The most common pattern in tennis elbow is improvement once total load is reduced and the tendon is strengthened over weeks to months, which is why medical guidance usually centers on rest from aggravating activity and graded exercise.
          </p>

          <p>
            See a doctor, sports medicine clinician or physical therapist if the pain is present at rest, keeps you awake, came on suddenly with a pop or swelling, involves numbness or tingling in the fingers, or has not improved after a few weeks of reduced play. Those can point to something other than simple overuse, and a professional can rule them out and set up a proper rehabilitation plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Braces and straps worn just below the elbow are widely used, and some players find they ease symptoms during play. Ask your clinician whether one suits you. They are a tool to manage symptoms, not a replacement for rest, strengthening and technique work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check the rest of your setup. Very hard outdoor balls, cold weather that stiffens both the ball and your arm, and long sessions all add load. Softer indoor balls, a proper warm-up and shorter sessions can help as much as a new paddle while you recover. Once you are back to full play, our guide to the <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddles</a> covers the wider field if you want to compare these picks with power and spin paddles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Buying Checklist
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose a thick or notably soft core first. A 16mm core is a sensible minimum for a sore elbow, and thicker cores like the 20mm LUXX Control Air are softer still. If you love a particular thinner paddle, look for consistent owner reports of a soft feel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep weight moderate, roughly 7.5 to 8.2 ounces for most players, and prefer a standard or hybrid shape if swing weight bothers you. Avoid very heavy, head-heavy or very light paddles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make sure the grip fits, ideally 4.125 inches or larger for most adult hands, and plan to add an overgrip. Look for a forgiving face, either a wide body or foam-filled edges, to reduce the jolt from off-center hits. If you play sanctioned events, confirm the exact model is listed; our guide to <a href="/usa-pickleball-approved-paddles/">USA Pickleball approved paddles</a> shows how to check.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then pair the paddle with technique changes and professional advice. The paddle is one part of the fix, and the cheapest part. If you also want spin from your new paddle, our guide to the <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-spin/">best pickleball paddles for spin</a> covers raw carbon options, several of which also come in softer 16mm versions.
          </p>

