        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Paddle to Cure the Elbow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A softer paddle reduces the load on the tendon but does not heal it. If you keep playing through pain at the same volume and with the same technique, the problem usually persists. Pair the paddle with rest, strengthening exercises and professional advice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Grip That Is Too Small</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A small grip makes you squeeze to control the paddle, which loads the tendon on every shot. Measure your hand, choose 4.125 inches or larger if it fits, and add an overgrip if your paddle runs small.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going Too Light or Too Heavy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A very light paddle tempts you to swing harder and tense up, while a very heavy or head-heavy one makes the forearm work to slow it down. Most players with elbow pain do best in the light-to-medium range.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Loading the Tip With Lead Tape</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lead tape at the tip of the paddle raises swing weight quickly and increases the work your forearm does on every swing. If you want stability, use small amounts at the sides or choose a paddle built a little heavier.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Persistent Pain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pain at rest, night pain, numbness, tingling or no improvement after a few weeks of reduced play are reasons to see a doctor or physical therapist. They can rule out other problems and set up a rehabilitation plan.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

