          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best pickleball paddle for tennis elbow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Our pick is the Selkirk LUXX Control Air Invikta. Retailers list a 20mm honeycomb core with a foam perimeter and a weight of 7.8 to 8.1 ounces, which makes it one of the softest, most cushioned paddles available. Budget buyers should look at the Selkirk SLK Halo Control Max or the Gamma Compass NeuCore.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a heavier or lighter paddle better for tennis elbow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most guidance points to the light-to-medium range, roughly 7.5 to 8.2 ounces. Heavier paddles absorb more impact but take more effort to swing and stop, while very light paddles can push you to swing harder and grip tighter. Swing weight matters too, so prefer a moderate one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are 16mm paddles better for tennis elbow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Thicker cores are generally described as softer and more cushioned, so 16mm or thicker is a sensible choice for a sore elbow. A few thinner paddles, like the Paddletek Tempest Wave Pro-C at 14.3mm, also have a reputation for a soft feel. Core construction and grip size matter as well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What grip size should I use for tennis elbow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A grip that lets you hold the paddle loosely, which for many adults means 4.125 inches or larger. A grip that is too small makes you squeeze, which loads the tendon. If you are between sizes, go larger, or add an overgrip to a smaller handle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a paddle cure tennis elbow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A paddle can reduce the shock, vibration and grip pressure that load the tendon, but recovery usually comes from reducing total load and strengthening the forearm over weeks to months. See a doctor or physical therapist if pain persists or appears at rest.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do thermoformed paddles cause tennis elbow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do not cause it on their own, but some arm-health guides report that certain foam-core and thermoformed paddles feel firmer and transmit more shock than traditional honeycomb paddles. If you have elbow pain, read owner reports from players with arm issues before buying one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I wear an elbow strap for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many players find a counterforce strap worn just below the elbow eases symptoms during play. Ask your doctor or physical therapist whether one suits you. A strap manages symptoms, but it does not replace rest, strengthening and technique changes.
              </p>
            </div>

